* Existing

** REPLACES/SUPPLEMENT:

- find_closing_brace(s, start) \\
  Designed to scan @(...} ; only called from expand_braces(), which is
  only invoked from dosubs().

  Returns: if brace found, a positive integer which is a position within s.
           if no brace/end of string, return EOF (0)
           or ERROR (-1) if things are really bad

- scan__usercmd_call() \\
  Implicitly scans $0 ; only called from parse(), which is invoked in
  lots of places.

  Returns: the command line, maybe after readline().  The final string
           is the ship_out'd as an OBJ_USER.


** IN ALL OF THE FOLLOWING FUNCTIONS
In the following 3 cases, invocation scanning (looking for a name and
zero or more brace-enclosing character sets) is only used for syntax
checking, building an array of argument values, probably remembering the
invocation name and the number of arguments.  But there is no need to
care about whatever value (or not) the scanning function returns, in
contrast to the above two items.

*** MIGHT BE USED FOR FIRST TIME TO HELP:
- parse__newcmd() \\
  Invoked from parse(). Used to scan @newcmd for parameter names;
  parameter names stored in blktab[NEWCMD_BLK, N, "param_name"], N >= 1.
  ``Function name'' stored in blktab[NEWCMD_BLK, 0, "name"]

  Returns: The function itself returns a block, but that's not related
           to brace parsing.  That's only for syntax checking/arg building.

- execute__user(name, cmdline) \\
  Invoked from ship_out().  When execute__user is called, dstblk is TERMINAL
  (zero) so everything gets executed.
  + Description \\
    Check for command line arguments; count in "narg", populate
    args[N].  args array is used to pass arguments to execute__user_body().

- dosubs() \\
  Look for symfun scanning for @foo{...}  Builds param[] array.

*** These three are very similar and are only invoked by dosubs().
They all return dosubs(result), obviously a substituted string used in
some output.

- xeq_fn__ifdef(fn, m, nparam, param) \\
  Checks to make sure there are 2 or 3 {} groups in m.
  { Var }{ True } [ { False } ]   . . . .
- xeq_fn__ifelse(fn, m, nparam, param) \\
  Similar to above, except needs at least 3.
  {a}{b}{eqtext}{...}
- xeq_fn__ifx(fn, m, nparam, param) \\
  Similar again, exactly 3 {} groups
  {cond}{true}{

* Array keys

Keys for invoc:
Leading @ is optional.
Name is optional.
If braces are being used, both opening and closing braces must be
present.  If braces are not used, both must be omitted.


- pos_ld_at :: pos of leading "@" if present; 0 if not
- pos_name :: pos of name, or 0 if omitted
- pos_obr :: pos of opening brace.  Required if pcbr != 0
- pos_stuff :: pos of first char of stuff.  See nargs and arg[]
- pos_cbr :: pos of closing brace.  Required if pobr != 0
- pos_tr_at :: pos of closing "@" if present; 0 if not

- valid :: bool
- name :: string or "" if absent
- nargs :: integer or 0 if no args
- arg[1..nargs] :: strings
- ld_at_p :: bool = ["pos_ld_at"] != 0
- tr_at_p :: bool = ["pos_tr_at"] != 0
- strval :: string

